---
// Fall week 2, Workshop 1: User Story Mapping (#466). A 50-minute session:
// talk for the first 15 minutes, teams map in the template until minute 45
// with the self-check up and a volunteer clinic at about minute 30, then the
// wrap. The talk slides' target times stay at 15:00 or less in total; the
// work-time and wrap slides carry the session clock in their cues. Every
// claim on a slide traces to a URL on the sources slide. The AI demo output
// box is a placeholder the instructor fills before class. Served at
// /decks/fall-week-2/.
import Notes from "../components/deck/Notes.astro";
import Slide from "../components/deck/Slide.astro";
import StoryMap from "../components/deck/StoryMap.astro";
import TitleSlide from "../components/deck/TitleSlide.astro";
import TwoColumnSlide from "../components/deck/TwoColumnSlide.astro";

export const title = "Fall Week 2: User Story Mapping";

// Shown twice, either side of the clinic, so the room keeps it in view.
const selfCheck = [
  "The backbone is what the user or the input does, never a screen or a component",
  "The walking skeleton has a story under every backbone column",
  "Every story is known, or starts with ? when it is assumed",
  "The backbone type is chosen, with one line saying why",
  "Every ? has a question on the Questions for our partner tab",
];
// The ephira day-one map: drawn once, shown on its own slide and again
// beside the AI tool's output.
const ephiraMap = {
  backbone: [
    "Get in safely",
    "Log a day",
    "Review the calendar",
    "Read cycle insights",
    "Get reminders",
    "Take the data out",
  ],
  releases: [
    {
      name: "Walking skeleton",
      stories: [
        "Set a password, unlock with it",
        "Log today's flow, edit a past day",
        "Logged days on a month view",
        "Current cycle day",
        "Daily reminder to log",
        "Delete all my data",
      ],
    },
    {
      name: "Release 1",
      stories: [
        "Biometric unlock, guided tour",
        ["Symptoms, moods, notes", "? Custom symptoms, medications"],
        "Filter the legend by entry type",
        "Predict after 2 cycles, phase tips",
        "? Reminder before a period",
        "? PDF export for a doctor",
      ],
    },
    {
      name: "Out of scope this year",
      stories: [
        "Color themes",
        "Pregnancy mode",
        undefined,
        undefined,
        "Birth control reminders",
        "Cloud backup",
      ],
    },
  ],
};
// Shown without its protocol, as the room would type it.
const templateUrl = new URL("/story-map-template.xlsx", Astro.site);
const template = `${templateUrl.host}${templateUrl.pathname}`;
---

<TitleSlide
  kicker="CS 461, fall week 2: Workshop 1"
  title="User Story Mapping"
  subtitle="A backbone, a release line, and a walking skeleton for your project"
  byline="CS 461: the instruction team"
>
  <Notes time="0:20" cue="Teams sit together by team number; one laptop per team opens the template.">
    <p>
      Fifteen minutes of talk, thirty minutes of mapping in teams, five
      minutes of wrap. By the end of class each team has a backbone, a
      walking-skeleton row, and its assumptions flagged.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</TitleSlide>

<Slide kicker="Four words for today" title="What a story map is made of">
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Backbone:</strong> the big activities across the top, in the
      order they happen
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Stories:</strong> the smaller steps under each activity, higher
      when more necessary
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Release line:</strong> a cut across the map; everything above it
      ships first
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Walking skeleton:</strong> the first slice, a story under every
      activity, so the whole thing runs end to end
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:30" cue="Press the arrow for each line; ask the room which line is the hardest to draw.">
    <p>
      Patton, whose article is on the next slide, does not prioritize the
      backbone: it is the set of things the system has to do at all. He
      prioritizes the stories hanging under it, moving them up or down, and
      lays tape across the map to make one lane per release.
    </p>
    <p>
      The stories placed highest form the smallest system that works end to
      end. Patton credits the term walking skeleton to Alistair Cockburn; use
      Patton's description here and don't paraphrase Cockburn's.
    </p>
    <p>
      One convention for today: a story nobody has checked with the project
      partner starts with a question mark, and the template turns it amber.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Where it comes from" title="Jeff Patton's map for Mimi">
  <ul>
    <li>Mimi: a Music Industry Marketing Interface, mapped in one day</li>
    <li class="fragment">
      The map narrowed the first release to one activity: a band member
      promoting a gig
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      Design the mailer, build the audience list, send it, track the
      responses
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      Mimi shipped; the other music features moved below the line
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:00" cue="One sentence on why a flat backlog loses this.">
    <p>
      From Patton's article "The New User Story Backlog is a Map". The product
      set out to do many music-industry things. Laying the whole thing out
      showed that one activity, promoting a gig, already took most of the
      map, and the rest looked like a tall order by comparison.
    </p>
    <p>
      Patton's complaint about a flat backlog is that it strips the context:
      a list in build order can't tell a stakeholder what the system does,
      and it can't show you what you missed. The map can, because you can
      walk it left to right with a user.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="A real scope cut" title="Allegro: two years of work, six months to ship">
  <ul>
    <li>A new shipment platform, integrated with a large ERP (enterprise resource planning) system</li>
    <li class="fragment">
      Estimated at about two years; the partner's release cycle allowed six
      months
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      Mapped as a whole team: a minimum viable product of 120 story points,
      against a pace of about 80
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      Cut and thinned stories to fit, built the API on mocks first so the
      partner could start
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">Released on time, with most of the features they wanted</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:15" cue="Ask: what is your partner's equivalent of the ERP release cycle?">
    <p>
      From the second part of Allegro's engineering blog series on delivering
      products. They combined a minimum viable product, a walking skeleton, and
      story mapping. The estimate after mapping still did not fit their
      velocity, so they cut the minimum viable product further and made some
      stories thinner.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first version gave no business value on its own: an API backed by
      mocks. It let the ERP partner start integrating, which was the real
      constraint. Your partner has clocks like that too.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Worked example: ephira, day one" title="The map a team could have drawn from the proposal" wide>
  <StoryMap {...ephiraMap} />
  <Notes time="2:00" cue="Point at the walking-skeleton row and read it left to right as one user's first day.">
    <p>
      ephira is an encrypted, local-first menstrual cycle tracker for iOS and
      Android, MIT licensed and published on both app stores. This is the map
      a team could have drawn in week 2, before knowing what the users wanted.
    </p>
    <p>
      The backbone names what the user does, never a screen: "Log a day", not
      "Day view". The walking skeleton is ugly on purpose: a password, one
      flow entry, a month view, a cycle day, one reminder, and a delete
      button. It runs end to end, and every later story hangs off something
      that already works.
    </p>
    <p>
      The three amber cards are the team's guesses. None of them is checked
      with a user yet, so each becomes a question for the partner.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<TwoColumnSlide kicker="Worked example: ephira, the reveal" title="What the first store version held" divider>
  <div slot="left">
    <h3>In the first store version</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Password and biometric lock; delete all data</li>
      <li>A full day log, the calendar and its filter, custom entries</li>
      <li>Color themes, which day one had ruled out</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div slot="right">
    <h3>Later, or never</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Later: predictions, cycle insights, PDF export, reminders, the guided tour</li>
      <li>Later: pregnancy mode, now a separate mode</li>
      <li>Never: cloud backup, since the data never leaves the device</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <Notes time="1:00" cue="Ask: which card on the day-one map turned out to be the hardest unknown?">
    <p>
      The first version the repository marks for the app store is 1.1.0: its
      app.json commit reads "Update version for appstore upload". At that
      commit the code has the password and biometric lock, the day view
      (flow, symptoms, moods, medications, birth control, notes), the
      calendar and its filter, custom entries, a theme selector, and delete
      all data. Its README still lists cycle prediction, data export, and
      cycle insight as open goals.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything on the right of this slide is in today's README and came in
      later versions. So the first release was thinner than the day-one map:
      the cycle-day and reminder stories in its walking skeleton were not
      there yet. Color themes moved up from out of scope; the line moves both
      ways. Pregnancy mode was started after the first release and now runs
      as its own set of tabs. Cloud backup is the deliberate one: the app
      promises data that never leaves the device, so backup would break the
      product's main promise. Writing it below the line, on purpose, settles
      the question before anyone asks for it.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</TwoColumnSlide>

<Slide kicker="Three backbones, one map" title="Choose the backbone where your hardest unknowns live">
  <div class="backbones">
    <div class="type">
      <h3>User journey</h3>
      <p>What a user does, in order</p>
      <p class="skeleton">Skeleton: one user gets through every activity once</p>
    </div>
    <div class="type fragment">
      <h3>Pipeline stages</h3>
      <p>What happens to one input on its way to an output</p>
      <p class="skeleton">Skeleton: one real input reaches an output, stubs allowed</p>
    </div>
    <div class="type fragment">
      <h3>Research chain</h3>
      <p>Question, data, baseline, method, evaluation, result</p>
      <p class="skeleton">Skeleton: the baseline runs end to end and produces one number</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="fragment rule-line">
    Same rows, release line, <code>?</code> flags, and partner questions for all
    three. Write one line saying why you chose yours.
  </p>
  <Notes time="1:00" cue="Ask for hands: who is not sure their project has a user?">
    <p>
      Why it matters: the walking skeleton should exercise the riskiest part
      of the project first, so the backbone has to run through it. In a
      recent year, about half the project listings had no clean user journey
      to map, so a good part of the room needs this slide.
    </p>
    <p>
      A game is a user journey with the player as the user; a library or an
      API is a user journey with the adopting developer as the user. Pick
      the backbone where the hardest unknowns are: if the scary part is
      whether a model beats a baseline, map the research chain, even if
      there is a web front end too.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Pipeline backbone: a sketch from the project page" title="Measuring waves from an ocean glider" wide>
  <StoryMap
    backbone={[
      "Sense",
      "Calibrate",
      "Spectral analysis",
      "Wave height on board",
      "Serial link to the glider",
      "Shore",
    ]}
    releases={[
      {
        name: "Walking skeleton",
        stories: [
          "Log raw accelerations",
          "Apply a bench calibration",
          "Wave spectrum on a laptop",
          "Wave height from one spectrum",
          "Send one value over serial",
          "? Glider relays it to shore",
        ],
      },
      {
        name: "Release 1",
        stories: [
          "? Sample within the power budget",
          "Model the glider's own motion",
          ["Directional spectrum", "Spectrum visualization"],
          "Spectrum computed on board",
          "? Serial API the glider accepts",
          undefined,
        ],
      },
    ]}
  />
  <p class="caption">
    Inferred from the project page, not the team's own map. The instruction
    team found no public repository.
  </p>
  <Notes time="1:15" cue="Ask: what is the input here, and who is the user?">
    <p>
      A capstone project with an oceanography lab: put an accelerometer
      package on an autonomous underwater glider and turn its readings into
      wave height, frequency, and direction. There is no user clicking
      through anything; the backbone is what happens to one burst of
      accelerations on its way to shore.
    </p>
    <p>
      The stages come from the project page's objectives and deliverables.
      The amber cards are what the page leaves open: the power budget of a
      low-power system at sea, and whether the glider takes the serial API.
      The instruction team found no public repository for this project.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Pipeline backbone: the walking skeleton" title="One thin path, sensor to shore, climbing a test ladder">
  <ol>
    <li>On a benchtop</li>
    <li class="fragment">In a ballast tank</li>
    <li class="fragment">In a wave flume with well-defined waves</li>
    <li class="fragment">In Yaquina Bay</li>
    <li class="fragment">In the open ocean near shore</li>
    <li class="fragment">In the open ocean beside a buoy with a calibrated wave sensor</li>
  </ol>
  <Notes time="0:45" cue="Ask: which rung would you be on at the end of fall?">
    <p>
      This ladder is in the project's own proposal. Read it as a walking
      skeleton: the same end-to-end path, from accelerations to one wave
      height on shore, runs at every rung, and each rung adds one kind of
      risk. A team that builds the full spectral package before the
      benchtop path works has nothing to take into the tank.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Research backbone: a sketch from the project page" title="Predicting a species' body mass from its name" wide>
  <StoryMap
    backbone={[
      "Question",
      "Data",
      "Baseline: genus mean",
      "Method",
      "Held-out error",
      "Result",
    ]}
    releases={[
      {
        name: "Walking skeleton",
        stories: [
          "Predict mass from a species name",
          "Measured masses, a small slice",
          "Mean mass of the species' genus",
          "Same as the baseline, a stub",
          "Error on species held out",
          "One number on the metric",
        ],
      },
      {
        name: "Release 1",
        stories: [
          "? Taxonomy beats the genus mean",
          "Whole database, fixed train and test split",
          undefined,
          ["? Fine-tuned language model", "? Tree-based model"],
          "Error by taxonomic rank",
          ["Estimate with uncertainty", "Single and batch queries on the web"],
        ],
      },
    ]}
  />
  <p class="caption">Inferred from the project page, not the team's own map.</p>
  <Notes time="1:15" cue="Ask: what single number would tell this team its method works?">
    <p>
      TaxonBodyMassML: ecologists have measured the body mass of thousands
      of species, a small fraction of all described species, and the
      project predicts the rest from the scientific name and taxonomy.
    </p>
    <p>
      The walking skeleton never touches the clever method. It takes a slice
      of the data, predicts each held-out species with its genus's mean, and
      reports one error number. Every method after that has a number to
      beat. The genus-mean baseline is this deck's choice, not something the
      project page names.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<TwoColumnSlide kicker="Research backbone: what happened" title="The method changed; the chain held" divider>
  <div slot="left">
    <h3>The proposal</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Fine-tune a large language model on name and mass pairs</li>
      <li>A web interface for single and batch queries</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div slot="right">
    <h3>The repository</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Fall: the train and test split</li>
      <li class="fragment">Winter: a gradient-boosted model on the taxonomy</li>
      <li class="fragment">That model is what shipped</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <Notes time="0:45" cue="Point at the Method column on the previous map: only that cell changed.">
    <p>
      The project page asked for a fine-tuned language model. The
      repository's history shows the data split in fall and an XGBoost
      regressor, a gradient-boosted tree model, in winter, and that model is
      what the team shipped. The backbone survived the change because the
      method was one column, not the whole map.
    </p>
    <p>
      The repository also shows packages and releases after the team's
      year. Those are the partner's maintenance; don't credit them to the
      team.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</TwoColumnSlide>

<Slide kicker="The other half of the room" title="Games and developer tools are user journeys too">
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A game:</strong> the player launches it, learns the controls,
      plays a level, fails and retries, and finishes
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>A library or an API:</strong> the adopting developer finds it,
      installs it, gets a first call working, integrates it, and upgrades
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="0:45" cue="Ask a game team to name its first backbone column out loud.">
    <p>
      For a game, the walking skeleton is one level, ugly, from launch to the
      end screen. For a library, it's a first call that works from a fresh
      install, documented in the README. The developer is the user, and
      "installs it" is an activity on the map like any other.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="AI demo: ephira's proposal" title="The same proposal, mapped by an AI tool">
  <pre class="prompt">Here is a software project proposal, as it was advertised to students:

[ADVERTISED PROJECT PROPOSAL]

Draft a user story map for the team's first release. Give a backbone of
four to seven activities in the order they happen, then a walking-skeleton
row with one story under every activity, then a Release 1 row and an
out-of-scope row. Keep each story under eight words.</pre>
  <Notes time="0:30" cue="Read the last two sentences aloud.">
    <p>
      Tool and model, named here only: [FILL IN THE TOOL AND MODEL YOU
      PRE-RAN]. Run it before class with ephira's proposal, as it was
      advertised, pasted in place of the placeholder, and keep the output as
      returned. The human map for the same proposal is the day-one map from
      earlier, shown again after the output.
    </p>
    <p>
      The prompt does not ask the tool to mark its assumptions, on purpose:
      the point is to see what it states as fact.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="AI demo: the output" title="What the tool drew from ephira's proposal">
  <div class="placeholder">
    <p class="placeholder-label">Placeholder</p>
    <p>Paste the pre-run tool output here</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="marks">
    <li><strong>Invented:</strong> [one story the proposal never mentions]</li>
    <li><strong>Stated as fact:</strong> [one assumption nobody has checked]</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:00" cue="Ask the room to find the invented story before you point at it.">
    <p>
      Placeholder: fill it before class. Replace the dashed box with the
      tool's output (an image, or a StoryMap), and fill the two bracketed
      lines with the story the tool invented and the assumption it stated as
      fact.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lesson for the room: the tool's map looks finished, which is the
      danger. Treat its output as a draft full of unflagged question marks,
      and check each one with the partner.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="AI demo: the human map" title="The day-one map for the same proposal" wide>
  <StoryMap {...ephiraMap} />
  <Notes time="0:30" cue="Go back one slide if the room wants to compare a column.">
    <p>
      The same map as the worked example. Point at one column where the tool
      and the team disagree, and at the amber cards: the human map flags its
      guesses, and the tool's map, asked the same way, did not.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Work time: minute 15 to 45" title="Self-check while you map">
  <ul class="checks">{selfCheck.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
  <p class="caption">Template: {template}</p>
  <Notes time="15:00" cue="Minute 15 to 30. Walk the aisles; at minute 30, move to the clinic slide.">
    <p>
      Teams open the template and map. The in-class target is the backbone,
      the walking-skeleton row, and the question marks. Release 1 can wait
      until after class.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two mistakes to look for as you walk: backbone columns named after
      screens or components ("Login page", "Database"), and a walking
      skeleton with gaps, which means it does not run end to end.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Clinic: minute 30" title="Put your backbone on the screen">
  <ul>
    <li>Two teams volunteer; two minutes of critique each</li>
    <li>The critique uses the self-check, nothing else</li>
    <li>If your project is under NDA, don't volunteer</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="5:00" cue="Minute 30 to 35. Then press left to put the self-check back up.">
    <p>
      Project the team's sheet from the lectern machine. Read the backbone
      left to right and ask the team to tell it as one story. Check the
      self-check in order: verbs, not screens; a skeleton story under every
      column; question marks where they're guessing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Name one thing that works before one thing to change. Two minutes
      each, hard stop, then back to work.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Work time: minute 35 to 45" title="Back to your map: the self-check">
  <ul class="checks">{selfCheck.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
  <p class="caption">Template: {template}</p>
  <Notes time="10:00" cue="Minute 35 to 45. At minute 45, move to the wrap.">
    <p>
      The same checklist as before the clinic. A team without a full
      walking-skeleton row by minute 40 should stop polishing Release 1 and
      fill the gaps, however uncertain the stories are.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Wrap: minute 45" title="Due by the end of the week">
  <ul>
    <li>One PDF of the whole workbook, both tabs: in Google Sheets, export the workbook, not the current sheet</li>
    <li>
      At least four backbone columns and one complete walking-skeleton row,
      however uncertain
    </li>
    <li>The backbone type, with one line saying why</li>
    <li>
      The Questions for our partner tab: the agenda for your first partner
      meeting
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="4:30" cue="Minute 45 to 49. End on the partner questions.">
    <p>
      The full map is due by the end of the week, as the Workshop Activities
      page says. A default PDF export from Google Sheets takes only the sheet
      on screen, so choose the whole workbook in the export dialog; the
      template prints each tab one page wide.
    </p>
    <p>
      The questions tab is the part to use this week: every amber card is a
      question, and the first partner meeting is where to ask them. Bring
      the map, walk it left to right with the partner, and move the line
      where they disagree.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Sources" title="Every claim in this deck, by URL">
  <ul class="sources">
    <li>
      Jeff Patton, The New User Story Backlog is a Map: <a href="https://jpattonassociates.com/the-new-backlog/">jpattonassociates.com/the-new-backlog/</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      Allegro Tech, building a minimum viable product with story mapping: <a href="https://blog.allegro.tech/2015/01/story-about-delivering-products-part2.html">blog.allegro.tech/2015/01/story-about-delivering-products-part2.html</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      ephira: <a href="https://github.com/adulbrich/ephira">github.com/adulbrich/ephira</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      Wave-sensing glider, project page: <a href="https://capstone.eecs.oregonstate.edu/projects/a75ffc21-b16f-5849-880a-d7667e6ae208">capstone.eecs.oregonstate.edu/projects/a75ffc21-b16f-5849-880a-d7667e6ae208</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      TaxonBodyMassML, project page: <a href="https://capstone.eecs.oregonstate.edu/projects/1e6e1a34-dee3-54bf-8c53-f876fb2d1519">capstone.eecs.oregonstate.edu/projects/1e6e1a34-dee3-54bf-8c53-f876fb2d1519</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      TaxonBodyMassML, repository: <a href="https://github.com/TaxonBodyMassML/TaxonBodyMassML">github.com/TaxonBodyMassML/TaxonBodyMassML</a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="0:30" cue="Leave up while teams pack.">
    <p>
      The backbones on the glider and TaxonBodyMassML slides are inferred
      from their project pages. The share of this year's listings that fit a
      story map is the instruction team's own count, so it stays in the
      notes.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<style>
  .backbones {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 48px;
  }

  .type {
    padding: 32px 36px;
